00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:41.230
Time to first token, or TTFT, is how long you wait before the model emits token 1, and

00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:46.270
most of that weight is pre-fill, writing the prompt into key value cash and GPU memory,

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.390
with decode only starting once that cash exists.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:55.110
Pre-fill costs scales with prompt length, which is why a fat-shared system prompt dominates

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:57.830
TTFT when every request pays it in full.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.050
The overlapping case is a long system prompt or the same retrieved document sitting in front

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:07.770
of many different user questions, and reuse only works if the next request hits the

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.510
worker that already has those keys and values resident.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:15.770
Kinference's balancer is built to send the request where that cache already lives, and

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:19.870
the median TTFT on that traffic fell to 0.31 seconds.

